schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Oracle (blockchain)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A service that feeds outside (off-chain) data to a blockchain, so a smart
    contract can react to real-world information it otherwise has no way to
    observe.
  source_traditions:
    - ethereum.org developer documentation on oracles
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Introduced to explain the limit of "a blockchain cannot verify the real
    world for you" — oracles are presented as a partial bridge, not a
    solution, since "someone still has to be trusted to report the truth in
    the first place."
  adjacent_meanings:
    - dMRV (digital measurement, reporting, and verification — an applied use of oracle-fed data for impact claims)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      An oracle moves the trust problem, it does not remove it — the chain
      only knows what the oracle told it, so oracle data quality/trustworthiness
      becomes the new bottleneck.
  tensions:
    - >-
      The article's own cautionary example (Toucan/Verra) shows that even with
      good-faith tokenization, the underlying real-world/regulatory truth can
      diverge from the on-chain record in ways an oracle alone does not prevent.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Presenting oracles as a full solution to "blockchain can't see the real
    world" understates the article's own point — oracles bridge the gap but
    still depend on trusted reporting.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-web3-can-cant-do.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
